Which model should we use?



What do we want to learn from the data?





Description: What was the churn rate last quarter?
Prediction: Which customers are likely to churn?
Statistical Inference: Does the observed rate represent the churn level of our customers well, or could it vary substantially from one sample to another?
Causal Inference: If we offer a benefit, by how much will churn decrease because of that intervention?



The question comes before the model.

4. Causal Inference: what would happen if we did something?

The question
Now the question changes again.
Companies do not want only to observe or predict the world. They want to act on it.
They:

change prices;
launch features;
send campaigns;
offer discounts;
change interfaces;
change processes;
create policies;
modify algorithms.

And then a different question arises:

What changed because of our action?

Examples:

If we offer a coupon, by how much do purchases increase?
If we change the checkout screen, by how much does conversion increase?
If we raise the price, how does demand change?
Did this campaign increase revenue?
Did this new onboarding reduce churn?

The fundamental expression here is:

because of.

We do not want to know simply whether two things appear together, but whether changing one of them causes a change in the other.


The Jump of the Cat: Association is not intervention
Imagine that customers who receive coupons buy more.
This does not necessarily mean that:

coupons caused those customers to buy more.

Perhaps the company offers coupons precisely to its best customers, who would already buy more even without the coupon.
Likewise:

users who use a certain feature have higher retention.

This does not necessarily mean that:

making a user use that feature will increase their retention.

Perhaps naturally more engaged users are precisely the ones who use the feature.
Causal Inference tries to answer:

What would have happened to those same units if the intervention had been different?

This is the idea of the counterfactual.


Prediction is not Causal Inference
Imagine two customers.
Customer A:

90% predicted probability of churn;
practically no response to a retention intervention.

Customer B:

40% predicted probability of churn;
their probability could fall to 10% if they received the intervention.

If the company looks only at prediction, it will probably prioritize A.
But if the question is:

“For whom will this intervention have the greatest impact?”

B may be the more interesting customer.
Therefore:

high predicted risk does not mean high causal effect.



What do we need to learn in order to do Causal Inference?
Causal Inference does not replace Statistical Inference. It builds on it and adds a new layer.
Besides the foundations of Probability and Statistical Inference, concepts such as these come in:

treatment;
outcome;
potential outcomes;
counterfactual;
treatment effect;
randomization;
confounding;
identification;
selection bias;
experiments and A/B tests;
observational data;
Difference-in-Differences;
Instrumental Variables;
Regression Discontinuity;
matching and weighting;
heterogeneous treatment effects;
Causal Machine Learning.

There is one particularly important distinction:
Statistical Inference helps answer:

“How precisely did we estimate this quantity?”

Causal Inference must first answer:

“Can this quantity be interpreted as a causal effect?”

Only after that do we estimate this effect and quantify its uncertainty.






Identification comes before estimation



An extremely precise confidence interval around the wrong quantity still does not answer the causal question.
Causal Inference requires not only estimating well, but also justifying why the estimated quantity represents the effect of the intervention we want to know.








Main idea



Causal Inference creates value when we need to discover which actions truly cause changes in the outcomes that matter to the company.

Does company size or business model matter?
Not necessarily.
Large digital companies often have particularly favorable conditions:

many users;
many interactions;
many repeated decisions;
good instrumentation;
experimentation infrastructure.

That is why we see companies such as iFood, Nubank, Amazon, Uber, Booking, and Netflix making intensive use of Machine Learning, Experimentation, and Causal Inference.
But company size, or whether it is B2B or B2C, does not by itself determine which questions can be asked.
A small company may have millions of observations, while a huge company may have little information for a specific question.
It is also important to remember:

number of rows is not necessarily the effective sample size.

A dataset may contain 20 million events produced by only a few hundred companies.
If the intervention occurs at the company level, the relevant number of units may be much smaller than 20 million.
What matters is the combination of:

business question;
unit of analysis;
unit of intervention;
amount of independent information;
data quality;
ability to measure outcomes;
economic value of the answer.

Why Probability?
We thus arrive at our initial question:

how can we mathematically represent a phenomenon whose individual outcome is uncertain, but which has structure and may exhibit regularity?

This is what we will construct Probability Theory for.
It will not eliminate uncertainty or necessarily tell us which outcome will occur. It will provide a language for stating precisely:

which outcomes are possible;
which sets of outcomes we want to study;
how we assign probabilities to them.

For now, just remember the story:
\[
\boxed{
\text{stochastic phenomenon}
\longrightarrow
\text{uncertainty + regularity}
\longrightarrow
\text{probability model}
}
\]

From the Random Experiment to the Statistical Space
In the previous chapter, we saw why we need Probability.
Now the formalization begins: we will turn the idea of an uncertain phenomenon into a mathematical structure that can later be used to model observed data.
Formalizing is not simply replacing words with symbols. It is learning to state precisely:
Modeling begins before choosing an estimator, testing a hypothesis, or running an algorithm.
The starting point is the Random Experiment: the conceptual mechanism we want to represent mathematically.
In Spanos’s framework, it brings together three ideas:
The formalization of these ideas will give rise to the Statistical Space.
\[
\boxed{
\text{Random Experiment}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{Statistical Space}
}
\]
In other words, the Random Experiment is not “inside” the Statistical Space. It is the conceptual phenomenon we are trying to represent mathematically.

[a] we know the possible outcomes a priori;
[b] in a particular trial, we do not know which outcome will occur, but there is probabilistic regularity;
[c] the experiment can be repeated under the same conditions.

Preview: when trials are IID
So far, \(\mathcal G_n\) represents only a sequence of trials.
Later, we will formalize the idea of repeating the experiment under the same conditions by imposing two properties:

Identical Distribution (ID): the same probabilistic structure is maintained from trial to trial;
Independence (I): information about one trial does not alter the probabilities of the others.


Just to be clear: in Mathematics and Statistics, imposing properties or making assumptions about a space or model means restricting the possibilities it allows. In other words, we are specializing a more general structure.
By imposing Identical Distribution and Independence on the Sampling Space, we obtain a simpler and more restrictive case of the Statistical Space.
Why do we do this? Because starting with a simple structure makes it easier for us to understand and study the phenomenon.
Later, when these assumptions are not appropriate, we can relax them and construct more general structures capable of representing dependence, heterogeneity, and other patterns found in the data.

Visually:
\[
\mathcal G_n
\quad\xrightarrow{\text{ID + Independence}}\quad
\mathcal G_n^{IID}.
\]
The sequence \(\mathcal G_n^{IID}\) will be called a sequence of Random Trials.
In this simple case, the Statistical Space takes the form
\[
\boxed{
\left[
(\Omega,\mathcal F,P)^n,
\mathcal G_n^{IID}
\right]
}
\]
which we will call the Simple Statistical Space.
We do not need to develop IID now.
For now, just remember the architecture:
\[
\boxed{
\text{Random Experiment}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{Statistical Space}
}
\]
with two parts:
\[
\boxed{
\text{Probability Space}
\;+\;
\text{Sampling Space}
}
\]
In the next chapters, we will open these boxes more carefully and then transform this abstract structure into objects suitable for working with data.

